Lets the core ask for the document that posted a page's notification (#679). The dispatcher
already keeps, per non-persistent notification id, the weak document and creator type its
listener was registered with, so the core no longer has to match the page's URL against the
tabs a profile holds, which a second tab on the same URL makes ambiguous. A worker's
notification has no document, and one whose document went away returns null; a document in
back/forward cache still counts, because the core counts a notification when Chromium
displays it, not when the page hears the show event. The public header's default returns
null, so the header alone still builds.

--- a/content/public/browser/notification_event_dispatcher.h
+++ b/content/public/browser/notification_event_dispatcher.h
@@ -15,6 +15,7 @@
 namespace content {
 
 class BrowserContext;
+class RenderFrameHost;
 enum class PersistentNotificationStatus;
 
 // This is the dispatcher to be used for firing events related to notifications.
@@ -64,6 +65,17 @@
   virtual void DispatchNonPersistentCloseEvent(
       const std::string& notification_id,
       base::OnceClosure completed_closure) = 0;
+
+  // browser_core: the document that posted the non-persistent notification
+  // identified by `notification_id`, or null for none it knows, a worker's or
+  // one whose document went away. The core counts a page's notifications on
+  // the tab of the document that posted them (#679), rather than matching the
+  // page's URL against the tabs it holds, which a second tab on that URL makes
+  // ambiguous.
+  virtual RenderFrameHost* GetNonPersistentNotificationDocument(
+      const std::string& notification_id) {
+    return nullptr;
+  }
 
  protected:
   virtual ~NotificationEventDispatcher() {}
